## Migration: ASP.NET WebForms → ASP.NET Core (.NET 8)

### Pattern Classification

Your code has two **ASP.NET request termination** patterns:

| Call | What it does in .NET Framework | Why it breaks |
|------|-------------------------------|---------------|
| `Response.End()` | Calls `Thread.Abort` internally | `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+ |
| `Response.Redirect(url, true)` | Aborts the thread after sending 302 | Same — `Thread.Abort` is gone |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Swallows the "expected" abort | Never thrown in modern .NET |

---

### Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

public class ReportController : Controller
{
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Export()
    {
        var data = GenerateReport();

        // Return a FileResult — the framework handles headers and response lifecycle.
        // No Response.End() needed; returning ends the action naturally.
        return File(
            System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data),
            "text/csv",
            "report.csv");
    }

    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Redirect()
    {
        if (!User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? true)
        {
            // Just return a redirect result — no thread abort, no endResponse flag.
            return RedirectToAction("Login", "Account");
            // Or: return Redirect("/login");
        }

        try
        {
            ProcessRequest();
            return Redirect("/success");
        }
        // No ThreadAbortException catch needed — it will never be thrown.
        // If ProcessRequest supports cancellation, catch OperationCanceledException instead.
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Request was cancelled (e.g., client disconnected)
            return StatusCode(499); // or just return empty
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            LogError(ex);
            return Redirect("/error");
        }
    }

    // If ProcessRequest is long-running, pass the cancellation token:
    private void ProcessRequest()
    {
        // For long-running work, accept HttpContext.RequestAborted:
        // private void ProcessRequest(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
        // {
        //     cancellationToken.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
        //     ...
        // }
    }
}
```

---

### Key Differences & Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Solution |
|---------|----------|
| Calling `Response.End()` or writing directly to `Response.Body` then expecting execution to stop | **Return an `IActionResult`** — the framework manages the response lifecycle. Execution after `return` never runs. |
| Using `Response.Redirect(url, true)` and relying on thread abort to stop execution | **Return `Redirect(url)`** — code after `return` won't execute. No abort needed. |
| Catching `ThreadAbortException` for flow control | **Remove entirely.** If you need to detect client disconnection, use `catch (OperationCanceledException)` with `HttpContext.RequestAborted`. |
| Writing response headers manually (`Response.AddHeader`) | Use `IActionResult` types (`FileResult`, `RedirectResult`) which set headers correctly. |
| Long-running work ignoring client disconnect | Pass `HttpContext.RequestAborted` as a `CancellationToken` through your call chain. |
| Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource` if you create linked tokens | Wrap in `using` to avoid timer/callback leaks. |

---

### Summary of Paradigm Shift

```
.NET Framework                    →  ASP.NET Core (.NET 8)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Response.End()                    →  return (from action method)
Response.Redirect(url, true)      →  return Redirect(url)
Response.Write(data)              →  return Content(data) / File(...)
catch (ThreadAbortException) {}   →  DELETE (or catch OperationCanceledException)
Thread.Abort for request kill     →  HttpContext.RequestAborted token
```

The fundamental change: **returning from an action method _is_ ending the response** in ASP.NET Core. There is no need to forcibly kill the thread.
